WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.609
On December 17th, 1903, on a beach at Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, two bicycle mechanics from Ohio flew the first powered airplane.

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:12.048
The flight lasted twelve seconds.

00:00:12.048 --> 00:00:15.817
The aircraft travelled one hundred and twenty feet.

00:00:15.817 --> 00:00:26.387
It was the first time in human history that a heavier-than-air machine, with a person aboard, lifted itself off the ground under its own power.

00:00:26.387 --> 00:00:39.692
Sixty-five years and seven months later — on July 20th, 1969 — Neil Armstrong stepped off the ladder of the Apollo 11 lunar module and put his boot down on the surface of the Moon.

00:00:39.692 --> 00:00:50.113
A person who, as a child, watched Orville Wright fly at Kitty Hawk was alive — and not even old — to watch Armstrong step onto another world.

00:00:50.113 --> 00:00:51.518
One human lifetime.

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:54.031
From sand dunes to lunar regolith.

00:00:54.031 --> 00:00:58.170
What it took to get there is on the map in front of you.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:00:59.870
Twelve crewed missions.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:00.831
Six landings.

00:01:00.831 --> 00:01:06.227
Twenty-four astronauts who circled the Moon, twelve of whom walked on it.

00:01:06.227 --> 00:01:12.805
A budget that consumed four percent of the United States federal expenditure at its peak.

00:01:12.805 --> 00:01:17.314
Four hundred thousand engineers, technicians, and scientists.

00:01:17.314 --> 00:01:28.401
Three crewmen — Grissom, White, and Chaffee — who never came home, killed by a fire on the launch pad before any of the rest of the program could fly.

00:01:28.401 --> 00:01:33.722
What Apollo left behind on Earth is harder to see, but it is everywhere.

00:01:33.722 --> 00:01:48.505
The integrated-circuit industry was a curiosity in 1961 when Kennedy announced the program — a paying customer for half the world's supply of microchips by the time the Eagle landed eight years later.

00:01:48.505 --> 00:01:57.227
Mission control software became the ancestor of every flight management system that flies a commercial airliner today.

00:01:57.227 --> 00:02:10.679
Materials, life support, kidney dialysis, freeze-drying, satellite communications, the cameras in your phone — every one of those technologies has a thread that runs through Houston.

00:02:10.679 --> 00:02:11.862
Look at the map.

00:02:11.862 --> 00:02:14.449
Six landing sites on the near side.

00:02:14.449 --> 00:02:16.001
Zero on the far side.

00:02:16.001 --> 00:02:17.331
Zero at the poles.

00:02:17.331 --> 00:02:21.471
Fifty-two years since the last footstep — until Artemis.

00:02:21.471 --> 00:02:24.501
Within one human lifetime, we went there.

00:02:24.501 --> 00:02:33.888
The question now — looking at the map and the years and the gap — is what the next lifetime does with what the last one taught.

